  {
    "episode_id": 90574,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90574.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 139,
    "turn_text": "We need more Americans to understand exactly this phenomenon. I've heard from senior producers at a couple of cable news networks. I've heard from senior acquisitions editors at magazines in New York that in a world where there's no chance in the world and there's no chance in hell you're getting a 70% audience for anything anymore. What you want is a deep and sticky 1% audience and one of the most effective ways to do that. You are the remnant. One of the most effective ways to do that is by getting attacked because you draw visibility to yourself. And so the Chick-fil-A nonsense, the New Yorker magazine nonsense about Chick-fil-A, saying that they were invading New York with some theonomic agenda to poison liberals or whatever crazy stuff they were saying. This is the New Yorker that has done some really important work, not just in its history, but this year. And at the same time, they write these just nonsensical, scurrilous pieces. And I think that the motive is to get attacked from the other side so you can now wear the victim badge of honor and that other people who are in your base then rally to you as a second order effect.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
