  },
  {
    "episode_id": 90574,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90574.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 172,
    "turn_text": "I mean, the average member of congress does take a pay cut. I'm sure to take these jobs, but it's still three times median household income in the US. So I don't think that incentives in that space are any kind of problem. I think people should want to go back to where they're from. And you should have people running who or desperate to get back home, but they do this as public service for a while, not as a way to get rich. But people do, if you look at, there's some finance profs around the country who've done longitudinal studies on this. People tend to have their nest egg grow a lot more while they're in Congress than happens to the average American. I didn't think about that. There's some big debates in the House about people sleeping in their office and whether or not there should be a way to do housing when you're here, because I do think we're better off with more members of Congress living where they're from rather than moving here. And yet, when they're here, it'd be useful for them to actually interact more to actually have some human relationships that bridge the screaming on cable. So I think there's some proposals in the House about housing allowance, but I don't have any carrots anywhere in our proposal.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
