  {
    "episode_id": 90574,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90574.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 173,
    "turn_text": "I get the political problem of proposing raising salaries on really reviled and hated people. But it's not something I'm passionate about. But when I hear people make this serious case about trying to maintain two residences, what they would make in the private sector in the first place, your services would be more compensated in other places other than the United States Senate. There's an argument there that sort of knee-jerk populists don't want to really give any credit to. And if you could really compensate these people to the point where you then said, that's it. There's no profiting off of this job whatsoever. We're paying you a really nice salary so that you don't take outside work. I wish I had that relationship with National Review, whether they pay me in chickens, which is why I have to do this podcast. But I think that there's an argument to me made there for it. But I agree with you. It's politically, it's a difficult thing to do, and I can see it from your perspective as well.",
